  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nElevated showers and thunderstorms will continue to move\nnortheast through central SD into the afternoon. Locally heavy\nrain and wind gusts to 50 mph will be the main threats.\n\nOtherwise, showers and thunderstorms will likely be hard to \ncome by this afternoon as strong capping is in place. The \nexception may be in central SD closer to increasing southwest \nflow aloft and slightly stronger surface heating. If these \nstorms can develop, CAPE around 2000 J/kg as well as about 40 \nknots of deep layer shear as well as decent turning from \nsoutheast to southwest in the lowest km. This will support all \nmodes of severe weather with an isolated tornado, hail to ping \npong ball and wind gusts to 70 mph. Unless the models are \ncompletely incorrect in handling the capping inversion, the \nchance for this very late afternoon and early evening convection\nwill be confined to areas west of the James River. One other\nnegative is the ongoing convection in central SD, which appears\nto be taking a bite out of the daytime heating.\n\nFarther east the threat will be more likely from about 8 pm to 2 am \nas storms could become more rooted in a stronger LLJ with increasing \nsouthwest flow aloft. This lay lead to a cluster of stronger\nupdrafts with half dollar sized hail, wind gusts to 60 mph and \npockets of heavy rain the main threats.\n\nInstability lingers through late tonight into early Tuesday \nmorning as upper level support slowly moves through. Confidence \nis low on additional stronger convection, but with potentially \n1000-1500 J/kg of instability some isolated stronger storms will\nbe possible. For now the threat of heavy rain like the past \ncouple of days looks pretty low as storms should be more \nprogressive.\n\nInstability is slow to shift southeast through the day on Tuesday \nand should result in additional showers and potentially stronger \nthunderstorms Tuesday afternoon. The better chance for this will \nlikely be south of a Yankton to Jackson MN line, with the better \nchance from about 3 pm to 8 pm.\n\nA stronger westerly jet aloft settles into the area Wednesday into \nThursday, with a fast moving trough expected to move through \nThursday night into Friday. This pattern should support dry \nconditions on Wednesday and Thursday with a chance for showers and \nthunderstorms late Thursday night, but more into Friday. Depending \nupon the timing of the front with this wave on Friday, highs could \nclimb into the 90s in a few locations before a fairly strong shot of \ncooler air moves in for next weekend where highs in the 70s are more \nlikely. Saturday night into Sunday will see a decent chance for \nshowers and thunderstorms as models are fairly agreeable with a wave \nmoving through, just not so much with the exact timing.",
